<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cyrus the Younger</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cyrus the Younger was a Persian prince of the late fifth century BCE, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-ii">Darius II</a>. In 401 BCE he hired Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in a bid to seize the throne from his brother Artaxerxes II, but was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cunaxa">Battle of Cunaxa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>'s account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>' retreat, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabasis">Anabasis</a>, exposed the weaknesses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-younger.jpg" alt="Cyrus the Younger" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_an_anonymous_Satrap_IONIA,_Phokaia._Circa_478-387_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
